+---
+
+# Symtropy Game Implementation Roadmap
+
+## Strategy
+
+Large carriers are a long-horizon promise. The near-term implementation proves the smallest architecture that preserves the promise without delaying Firstlight and the Continuance Crawler.
+
+# Gate A — Preserve the Playable Spine
+
+Complete movement, physical interaction, unique residents, the Breaking and one drivable mobile home.
+
+**Exit:** the player has a place and people worth carrying.
+
+# Gate B — Carrier Data Kernel
+
+Implement carrier identity, configuration epochs, district graph and twelve common-core capacity domains.
+
+**Exit:** one fixed carrier profile validates and replays.
+
+# Gate C — One Hull, Three Profiles
+
+Use one hull to instantiate habitation, survey and relief emphases.
+
+**Exit:** capacity and workflow differ while all common-core domains remain useful.
+
+# Gate D — One Nested Chain
+
+Embark a Continuance crawler aboard one medium base-carrier and one large carrier.
+
+**Exit:** identity, residents, cargo, damage and authority survive deployment and recovery.
+
+# Gate E — Station and Delegation Proof
+
+Implement one navigation, one engineering and one harbor station with qualified NPC delegation.
+
+**Exit:** single-player and two-player operation work from the same state.
+
+# Gate F — Ownership and Authority Ledger
+
+Separate title, module claims, debt, operational authority, command and resident governance.
+
+**Exit:** a player can command without owning and own without unlimited command.
+
+# Gate G — Graceful Degradation
+
+Implement radiator loss, dock isolation, clinic surge and command succession.
+
+**Exit:** the carrier survives with changed capability and persistent damage.
+
+# Gate H — Construction Program Proof
+
+Implement one charter, yard, two supply chains, labor requirement, defect, trial and commissioning milestone sequence.
+
+**Exit:** the player can materially shape a carrier build without inventory grind.
+
+# Gate I — Variety Proof
+
+Add a second and third hull lineage, internal topologies and sensory identities.
+
+**Exit:** blind reviewers distinguish ships without labels or color.
+
+# Gate J — Representative Benchmark
+
+Run six profiles, nested chain, four ownership forms, single/co-op emergency and performance evidence.
+
+**Exit:** all promotion gates pass and limitations are published.
+
+# Deferred
+
+- fully simulated populations of thousands at local AI detail;
+- ten production-ready carrier families;
+- open-ended hull generation;
+- interstellar seed-ark gameplay;
+- complete naval and ocean carrier simulation;
+- fleet warfare at civilization scale;
+- player-built Atlas Gate Tenders;
+- alien-grown carrier construction campaigns.
+
+# Kill Rules
+
+- Cut carrier count before cutting common-core truth.
+- Cut direct station detail before making NPC crew incompetent.
+- Cut construction breadth before turning it into resource grind.
+- Cut visual modularity before accepting one universal best hull.
+- Do not make ownership the only path to meaningful play.
+- Do not let carrier work delay the first compelling mobile-home experience.
+
+## Roadmap Maxim
+
+> **First prove that one home can be carried without losing itself. Then prove that one large ship can contain many homes without pretending it owns them.**
