+# v2.9 Player-Built Carrier Civilizations, Nested Vehicle Ecology, and Command at Scale Campaign
+
+## Campaign Question
+
+Can Symtropy make civilization-scale spacecraft player-buildable and commandable while keeping them varied, multirole, inhabited, politically legitimate and physically connected to smaller mobile homes?
+
+## Canonical Decisions
+
+1. Large carriers are broad-spectrum civilizations, not single-role classes.
+2. A common civic core is mandatory; mission profiles allocate emphasis.
+3. Shared interfaces enable interoperability without visual sameness.
+4. Hull lineages, internal city plans, cultures and repair ancestry provide variety.
+5. Large ships carry smaller base-carriers, which carry mobile homes and field vehicles.
+6. Nested homes preserve identity, people, cargo, ownership, damage and memory.
+7. Access begins early; qualification, authority, ownership and command progress separately.
+8. Ownership is usually shared, public, cooperative, chartered, financed or contested.
+9. Construction is a playable industrial and political campaign, not handheld crafting.
+10. Command coordinates departments and autonomous people; it is not absolute control.
+11. Automation compresses routine while preserving human and machine agency.
+12. Damage creates partial survival, changed mission and history.
+13. Ocean, hostile-world, route, care, trade and foundry carriers remain multirole.
+14. The first implementation proves one hull, three profiles and one nested chain.
+
+## Patch Sequence
+
+1. Multirole carrier civilization and non-specialization contract.
+2. Common core, open interfaces and role-envelope runtime with schema.
+3. Hull lineages, internal cities and cultural variety atlas.
+4. Nested vehicle ecology, docking and deployment runtime.
+5. Access, ownership, charter, command and crew sovereignty contract.
+6. Construction yards, supply chains, certification and commissioning runtime.
+7. Crew, automation, stations, damage and graceful degradation runtime.
+8. Ten multirole carrier families and world adaptation atlas.
+9. Benchmark, schemas, fixture and 100-ticket backlog.
+10. Registry, dependency, roadmap, readiness and release integration.
+
+## Difficulty Answer
+
+| Relationship | Intended difficulty |
+|---|---|
+| Board and live aboard | early and accessible |
+| Perform useful supervised work | early-mid with training |
+| Become independent operator | substantial mastery |
+| Lead a department or carried base | major trust and responsibility |
+| Command a mission | late-mid or late, legitimacy-dependent |
+| Own a meaningful share | difficult but reachable through several economic paths |
+| Hold sole title to a large carrier | rare, expensive and still bounded by rights |
+| Build a medium base-carrier | settlement-scale campaign |
+| Build a civilization carrier | late-game, multi-institution, often multi-generational campaign |
+| Absolutely control all people aboard | never canonical |
+
+## Evidence Boundary
+
+These files are D3 / I0 documentation. They do not prove large-ship physics, player construction, procedural variety, docking, crew behavior, command UX, networking or fun.
+
+## Campaign Maxim
+
+> **The player does not earn a large carrier by becoming rich enough to purchase a city. They earn a place in its future by learning how to help a city move.**
